WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.006
Origin Dossier starts where the character sheet stops.

2
00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:12.011
It takes the events that shaped a runner and turns them into things the table can actually use: contacts, enemies, debts, scars, secrets, beliefs, and unfinished consequences.

3
00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:18.017
The player keeps control. The GM keeps the campaign steer. Nothing becomes part of the game until both sides approve it.

4
00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:24.022
A clinic favor can become pressure. A family name can become a lead. A mistake can become a secret.

5
00:00:24.022 --> 00:00:30.028
A scar can become a code the runner lives by.

6
00:00:30.028 --> 00:00:36.034
The dossier can also feed portraits, scene packets, narration, video, and audiobook versions later, but the mechanics stay in Chummer.

7
00:00:36.034 --> 00:00:42.039
Prose never silently changes ware, money, qualities, magic, legality, or build math.

8
00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:48.045
When ALICE reads approved origin material later, it reads character context, not hidden rules.

9
00:00:48.045 --> 00:00:54.050
Weak media can be rejected without damaging the runner. Strong material gives the crew a person to bring into the next job.

10
00:00:54.050 --> 00:01:00.056
The best version feels intimate without becoming invasive.

11
00:01:00.056 --> 00:01:06.062
It gives the GM handles, gives the player choices, and keeps approval visible so nobody confuses generated drama with accepted canon.

12
00:01:06.062 --> 00:01:12.067
The result should feel like a campaign artifact, not a profile card: concise, dramatic, useful, and still open enough for the player to surprise everyone at the table.

13
00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:18.073
A good dossier knows when to stop.

14
00:01:18.073 --> 00:01:24.078
It should reveal enough pressure to make play richer, but leave enough air for the player to make new choices at the table.

15
00:01:24.078 --> 00:01:30.084
Not a backstory pasted on top. A life with consequences, ready for the next scene.
